# Visualization of Multivariate Data

<h2> Panel displays and correlation plots </h2>
<h2> Surface plots, 3D scatter plots, contour plots, 2D histograms </h2>
<h2> Andrews curves, parallel coordinates, segment plots </h2>
<h2> Principal components analysis and biplots </h2>

::: notes
- Rizzo Chapter 5. The theme: we can only see 2 dimensions on a screen, so every method here is a projection, a slicing, or an encoding of p > 2 dimensions.
:::

## The Problem

- Data $\bX$ is $n\times p$; we see 2 (maybe 3) dimensions at a time.
- Strategies:
  + **Many low-dimensional views:** scatterplot matrices, panels (conditioning)
  + **Encode variables:** color, shape, size, curves (Andrews), lines (parallel coordinates), glyphs (segments, stars)
  + **Functions of two variables:** surfaces and contours (densities!)
  + **Projections:** principal components

## Example 5.1: Scatterplot Matrix (Panel Display)




::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
pairs(iris[, 1:4], col = c("#C0392B", "#2E5BBA", "#27AE60")[iris$Species], pch = 20,
      upper.panel = function(x, y, ...) { usr <- par("usr"); on.exit(par(usr = usr)); par(usr = c(0, 1, 0, 1))
        text(.5, .5, sprintf("r = %.2f", cor(x, y)), cex = 1.6) })
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](./images/08-visualization/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::




## Conditioning Plots with `lattice`




::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(lattice)
splom(~ iris[, 1:4] | Species, data = iris, pscales = 0, varname.cex = 0.8,
      main = "Scatterplot matrices conditioned on species", layout = c(3, 1))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](./images/08-visualization/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::




## Example 5.2: Correlation Plots




::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
R <- cor(USJudgeRatings)
ord <- order.dendrogram(as.dendrogram(hclust(as.dist(1 - R))))
levelplot(R[ord, ord], at = seq(-1, 1, .1), col.regions = colorRampPalette(c("#2E5BBA", "white", "#C0392B"))(40),
          scales = list(x = list(rot = 90)), xlab = "", ylab = "", main = "USJudgeRatings correlations (clustered order)")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](./images/08-visualization/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::




::: tip
`corrplot::corrplot(R, order = "hclust")` produces the circle/ellipse displays shown in the book (decathlon data, `FactoMineR`).
:::

## Example 5.3: Bivariate Normal Density with `persp`




::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
f <- function(x, y, rho = 0.6) exp(-(x^2 - 2 * rho * x * y + y^2) / (2 * (1 - rho^2))) / (2 * pi * sqrt(1 - rho^2))
x <- y <- seq(-3, 3, length = 50)
z <- outer(x, y, f)
par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(1, 1, 1, 1))
persp(x, y, z, theta = 45, phi = 30, expand = 0.6, ltheta = 120, shade = 0.75,
      ticktype = "detailed", col = "#FFCC00", border = NA, xlab = "X", ylab = "Y", zlab = "f(x, y)")
persp(x, y, z, theta = -30, phi = 15, expand = 0.6, col = "lightblue", border = "gray40")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](./images/08-visualization/unnamed-chunk-5-1.png){fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::




## Example 5.4: Adding Elements to a Perspective Plot

`persp()` returns the viewing transformation; `trans3d()` maps 3-d points to the plot.




::: {.cell layout-align="center"}

```{.r .cell-code}
par(mar = c(1, 1, 1, 1))
M <- persp(x, y, z, theta = 45, phi = 30, expand = 0.5, col = "gray95", border = "gray80", box = FALSE)
# add a contour of f at its 50% level and the density along the line y = x
cl <- contourLines(x, y, z, levels = 0.08)[[1]]
lines(trans3d(cl$x, cl$y, rep(0.08, length(cl$x)), M), lwd = 3, col = "#C0392B")
s <- seq(-3, 3, length = 100)
lines(trans3d(s, s, f(s, s), M), lwd = 3, col = "#003366")
points(trans3d(0, 0, f(0, 0), M), pch = 19, cex = 2)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](./images/08-visualization/unnamed-chunk-6-1.png){fig-align='center' width=100%}
:::
:::




